A||N||5||rnoswal||2.8 heads||||1||
Z||000000||rnoswal||02-20-2001||10:55 PM||||I was just wondering if the aluminum heads on the 3.1's will fit the 2.8 that had the cast iron heads. Does this require different pistons because of combustion chamber size, or are they the same?<P>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000001||David Gamache||02-20-2001||11:08 PM||||The aluminum heads will fit the 2.8L. The 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 are all from the same family of GM blocks (late 87-88 blocks flow oil better as do the 3.1 and 3.4, but the crank journals on the 2.8 can be modified to flow just as well). The dish pistons are used in place of the flat pistons with the aluminum heads. A different thickness head gasket is required as well as a different intake, valve covers,injectors and fuel rail. The aluminum heads are second generation and have the larger valves like the 88 and newer performance cast iron heads and the valves on the aluminum heads are angles with a much better flow. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||02-20-2001||11:13 PM||||not easy to do. need  3.1 intake+ext manafolds custom pistons and other parts plus rewire for diffrent computer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rnoswal||02-20-2001||11:23 PM||||My plan is to do away with the fuel injection and go carburetor. Bigger cam too. Any idea how high the compression ratio will be with the flat 2.8 pistons with the aluminum heads? Any cc's for either the 3.1 or 3.4 heads? <P>Thanks<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||02-21-2001||09:14 PM||||the al heads are a diffrent casting<BR>with diff ports, smaller cumbustion<BR>chamber size and shape.intake manfold<BR>for iron head won't fit,and no carb<BR>manfold is made for al heads that I <BR>know of.allso al heads use roller lifter<BR>cam but no hi-po cams are made for them.<BR>you may need custom pistons to use 2.8<BR>crank.<BR>I wish the heads were swapable as al is<BR>lite and cools better but this an't easy<BR>swap to do, 3.4 DOHC may be cheaper to do.<BR>||||reg||1
